## 1. System Overview

The repo is a **desired-state configuration store**, not a live mirror of any machine. `main` describes what every machine's configuration *should* be. Each machine runs a reconciliation flow that pulls that desired state and converges its local filesystem toward it via GNU Stow. Machine-specific changes flow back into Git only through an explicit, human-gated capture step — never automatically.

```
                         ┌───────────────────────┐
                         │   GitHub: main         │
                         │   (desired state)      │
                         └───────────┬───────────┘
                                     │ fetch / pull
                         ┌───────────▼───────────┐
                         │   staging              │
                         │  (integration branch)  │
                         └───────────┬───────────┘
                                     │ rebase
                    ┌────────────────┼────────────────┐
                    ▼                ▼                ▼
             machine/laptop   machine/desktop   machine/wsl-dev
                    │                │                │
              ┌─────▼─────┐    ┌─────▼─────┐    ┌─────▼─────┐
              │ bootstrap.sh│   │ bootstrap.sh│   │ bootstrap.sh│
              │ stow.sh     │   │ stow.sh     │   │ stow.sh     │
              └─────┬─────┘    └─────┬─────┘    └─────┬─────┘
                    ▼                ▼                ▼
               $HOME (linux)    $HOME (windows)   $HOME (wsl)
```

**The one sentence that governs every design decision below:**

> Git describes desired configuration. The machine owns its own runtime state. Nothing crosses from machine → Git without an explicit, reviewed step.

This directly replaces the original proposal's model, where hardware events (battery/power) triggered an unconditional `git add . && commit && push` from the machine back into a branch. That model is retired — see §6 and §8 for what replaces it.

