## 7. Reconciliation & Sync Flow

This is the flow that replaces the original proposal's battery-triggered `git add . && commit && push`.

### 7.1 One-way sync (machine converges to Git) — automatic, unattended

```
Trigger (see 7.3)
   │
   ▼
DISCOVER   — read local state, read desired state from staging
   │
   ▼
PLAN       — compute what would change to converge local → desired
   │
   ▼
DIFF       — show the plan (logged; visible via a status command)
   │
   ▼
APPLY      — re-stow, apply config changes
   │
   ▼
VERIFY     — confirm the applied state matches desired state
   │
   ▼
REPORT     — write state/last-sync.json (observed state, local only)
```

This direction never writes to Git. It only pulls.

### 7.2 Drift capture (machine → Git) — explicit, human-gated

```
Machine discovers drift (local config differs from what's in Git)
   │
   ▼
Reconciler reports it (does NOT auto-commit)
   │
   ▼
Human/agent reviews the diff
   │
   ▼
Explicit capture — the reviewed subset only, staged against
the allow-list in policy/ (never a bare `git add .`)
   │
   ▼
Commit on machine/<hostname>
   │
   ▼
PR → CI → main
```

This is the direct fix for the audit's "automatic commits need a policy" finding. `git add .`/`git add -u` across the whole tree is retired as a sync mechanism entirely; staging is always scoped to an explicit path allow-list defined in `policy/`.

### 7.3 Trigger hierarchy

```
TIMER (systemd timer / Task Scheduler, every 15–30 min)   ← primary, safety-net trigger
LOGIN / BOOT                                                ← convenience trigger
MANUAL (`dotctl reconcile`)                                 ← on demand
POWER / NETWORK EVENT (udev, WMI)                           ← optional accelerator only
```

The timer is the trigger the system's correctness depends on; everything else exists to make convergence *feel* faster, never to be the only path to convergence. This directly resolves the audit's objection to battery-percentage as a primary trigger (`POWER_SUPPLY_CAPACITY=="50"` fires on noisy, frequent transitions) — event triggers now just call the same idempotent `reconcile` operation early, with a lock (`flock`) preventing overlapping runs.

