## 10. Reconciler Interface

The target operator surface is a single command, wrapping the scripts that already exist rather than replacing them:

| Command | Wraps today | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| `dotctl discover` | (new) | Detect platform/environment, resolve host manifest |
| `dotctl plan` | `stow.sh -n` (dry-run) | Show what would change |
| `dotctl diff` | (new, thin) | Show desired vs. observed state |
| `dotctl apply` | `stow.sh` | Apply the plan |
| `dotctl verify` | `validate.sh` | Confirm applied state is correct |
| `dotctl status` | (new) | Report current state + last sync time |
| `dotctl drift` | (new) | Report divergence without applying (§7.2 entry point) |
| `dotctl reconcile` | `pull-updates.sh` | Run the full one-way sync flow (§7.1) |

`dotctl` is introduced as a thin wrapper first — it should not become a rewrite of `stow.sh`/`validate.sh`/`bootstrap.sh`. Those scripts remain the actual implementation; `dotctl` gives them one consistent, memorable entry point and a place to add `plan`/`diff`/`drift` semantics that don't exist as isolated scripts today.

